// What the screens say about each weekly portion of Genesis (Bereshit). One entry per portion:
//   meaning   what the portion's name means (or, for a portion named after a person, who: who they are)
//   summary   what happens, in plain English
//   lessons   its main lessons (the left screen shows four), each with where it comes from
//   days      one insight per section (aliyah), Sunday to Shabbat. "verse" is the verse it's about, which
//             the right screen shows with Rashi and the Talmud on it; it has to be inside that day's section.
window.TORAH_LESSONS = window.TORAH_LESSONS || {};
Object.assign(window.TORAH_LESSONS, {

  'Bereshit': {
    meaning: 'In the beginning',
    summary: 'God creates the world in six days and rests on the seventh. Adam and Eve live in the Garden of Eden until they eat from the forbidden tree and are sent out. Their son Cain kills his brother Abel. Ten generations later the world is full of wrongdoing, but one man, Noah, finds favor with God.',
    lessons: [
      { title: 'Every person is made in God’s image', text: 'The Torah begins with all of humanity, not one nation. If every person carries God’s image, every life is worth everything. The Mishnah says that’s why the world began with one person: no one can claim a greater ancestor.', source: 'Genesis 1:27 · Mishnah Sanhedrin 4:5' },
      { title: 'Rest is part of creation', text: 'After six days of making, God stops and makes the seventh day holy. Shabbat says we are more than what we produce. Stopping isn’t quitting; it’s how the work becomes complete.', source: 'Genesis 2:1–3' },
      { title: '“Where are you?”', text: 'After eating the fruit, Adam hides, and God calls out: “Where are you?” God knew. The question is for Adam, and for us: change starts when we stop hiding and say where we really are.', source: 'Genesis 3:9' },
      { title: 'We are our brothers’ keepers', text: 'Cain asks, “Am I my brother’s keeper?” The rest of the Torah answers yes. And before the murder, God told Cain he could master his anger: the urge is real, but so is the choice.', source: 'Genesis 4:7–9' },
    ],
    days: [
      { verse: 'Genesis 1:31', headline: 'Notice what’s good before you fix what’s wrong.', text: 'Six times God looks at what was made and sees that it is good. At the end, looking at everything together, it is “very good.” The parts were fine; the whole was better. Before you start correcting things today, take stock of what’s already working.', try: 'Name three things that are going right, out loud.' },
      { verse: 'Genesis 2:18', headline: 'Nobody is meant to do this alone.', text: '“It is not good for man to be alone” is the first thing in creation God calls not good. The answer is an <i>ezer kenegdo</i>, “a helper opposite him”: not a servant, but a partner who can push back. The people who challenge you are part of the help.', try: 'Reach out to someone you haven’t talked to in a while.' },
      { verse: 'Genesis 3:12', headline: 'Don’t pass the blame.', text: 'Asked what happened, Adam blames Eve, and, slyly, God: “the woman You put at my side.” Eve blames the serpent. Nobody says “I did it.” Rashi calls Adam ungrateful here. The way back starts with owning our part.', try: 'Own one mistake out loud today, with no “but.”' },
      { verse: 'Genesis 4:7', headline: 'You can master it.', text: 'Before Cain acts, God warns him: sin is crouching at the door, it wants you, “but you can rule over it.” The Torah’s first conversation about temptation is also its first promise of free will. The feeling isn’t the choice.', try: 'When something sets you off today, wait ten seconds before you answer.' },
      { verse: 'Genesis 4:21', headline: 'Make something beautiful.', text: 'In a reading only four verses long, the Torah names the first herdsman, the first musician, and the first metalworker. Right after the first murder, people start building culture: shelter, music, tools. Making things is part of being made in the image of the Maker.', try: 'Spend ten minutes making something: music, a meal, a sketch.' },
      { verse: 'Genesis 5:1', headline: 'Everyone is in the same family book.', text: 'Rabbi Akiva said “love your neighbor as yourself” is the Torah’s great principle. Ben Azzai pointed to this verse instead, “This is the record of Adam’s line,” because it reminds us every person comes from the same first human, made in God’s likeness.', try: 'Treat someone you find hard to like as a relative.' },
      { verse: 'Genesis 6:8', headline: 'One person can change the story.', text: 'The portion ends with the world going wrong: every plan in people’s hearts “nothing but evil all the time.” Then one line: “But Noah found favor with the LORD.” A whole world’s future hangs on one decent person. Your choices are never too small to matter.', try: 'Do one kind thing nobody will see.' },
    ],
  },

  'Noach': {
    who: 'Noah, whose name means “rest”',
    summary: 'The world is full of violence, so God brings a flood. Noah, the one righteous man of his time, builds an ark and saves his family and the animals. Afterward God promises never to destroy the world again, with the rainbow as a sign. Later, people build the Tower of Babel and are scattered, and the portion ends with the family of Abram.',
    lessons: [
      { title: 'Righteous in his time', text: 'Noah was “righteous in his generation.” The rabbis argued over that phrase: maybe he’d have been even greater in a better time, or maybe he only looked good next to the wicked (Rashi). Either way, we’re measured in the times we live in.', source: 'Genesis 6:9 · Rashi' },
      { title: 'Violence ruins the world', text: 'The flood comes because “the earth was filled with <i>chamas</i>”: violence and lawlessness. The Talmud says the final decree was sealed over robbery. How we treat each other is what holds the world together.', source: 'Genesis 6:11–13 · Sanhedrin 108a' },
      { title: 'A promise in the clouds', text: 'After the flood God makes a covenant with every living thing, not only Noah’s family, and sets the rainbow as its sign. God commits to the world even knowing people will fail again.', source: 'Genesis 9:8–17' },
      { title: 'The trouble with Babel', text: 'The builders of Babel want to “make a name” for themselves with a tower to the sky: one language, one plan, no room for anyone different. God scatters them into many peoples. Difference isn’t the punishment; it’s the plan.', source: 'Genesis 11:1–9' },
    ],
    days: [
      { verse: 'Genesis 6:22', headline: 'Do the whole thing, not most of it.', text: 'Noah gets detailed instructions: the wood, the size, the window, the door, the food. The section ends: “Noah did so; just as God commanded him, so he did.” No skipping the boring parts. A big job done completely beats a brilliant one done halfway.', try: 'Finish one task you’ve left 90% done.' },
      { verse: 'Genesis 7:7', headline: 'Don’t wait for the water to rise.', text: 'Noah goes into the ark “because of the waters of the flood.” Rashi says Noah only half believed the warning: he didn’t go in until the water forced him. Even good people put things off until there’s no choice.', try: 'Do the thing you’d do if the deadline were today.' },
      { verse: 'Genesis 8:1', headline: 'You are not forgotten.', text: 'After 150 days of water, “God remembered Noah and all the animals.” The turning point of the flood isn’t the storm stopping; it’s being remembered. In a stretch that feels endless, the water starts going down long before you can see dry land.', try: 'Tell someone you’re thinking about them.' },
      { verse: 'Genesis 9:6', headline: 'Every person is a picture of God.', text: 'Starting over after the flood, God gives Noah’s family the most basic law: do not take a human life, “for in His image God made man.” Harming a person damages something holy. The world’s restart is built on that one idea.', try: 'Look for God’s image in someone who annoys you.' },
      { verse: 'Genesis 9:13', headline: 'Look for the sign after the storm.', text: 'A rainbow appears when sunlight meets rain: after a storm, not instead of one. God makes it the sign of a promise that the world won’t be destroyed again. Hard days still come; they just don’t get the last word.', try: 'Notice one small sign today that things are holding together.' },
      { verse: 'Genesis 9:23', headline: 'Cover someone’s shame. Don’t spread it.', text: 'When Noah gets drunk and lies uncovered, his son Ham looks, then goes and tells his brothers. Shem and Japheth walk in backwards with a cloak and cover their father without looking. Same facts, two choices: broadcast it, or protect a person’s dignity.', try: 'Don’t repeat the gossip you hear today.' },
      { verse: 'Genesis 11:4', headline: 'Build something without needing a name.', text: 'The builders of Babel say, “Let us make a name for ourselves.” A midrash says that when a brick fell they wept, but when a worker fell they didn’t notice (Pirkei deRabbi Eliezer 24). A project that matters more than its people has lost the plot.', try: 'Thank someone whose work usually goes unseen.' },
    ],
  },

  'Lech Lecha': {
    meaning: 'Go forth',
    summary: 'God tells Abram to leave his land and his father’s house for a land God will show him, promising that his family will become a great nation and a blessing to the world. Abram and Sarai travel to Canaan, go down to Egypt in a famine, part ways with Abram’s nephew Lot, and rescue Lot in a war. God makes a covenant with Abram, Hagar gives birth to Ishmael, and Abram and Sarai get new names: Abraham and Sarah.',
    lessons: [
      { title: 'Go, even without a map', text: '“Go from your land… to the land that I will show you.” Abram leaves without knowing where he’s going. Growing often means leaving what’s familiar before the destination is clear.', source: 'Genesis 12:1' },
      { title: 'Be a blessing', text: 'God promises Abram, “You shall be a blessing,” and that all the families of the earth will be blessed through him. Being chosen isn’t about being better. It’s a job: making other people’s lives better.', source: 'Genesis 12:2–3' },
      { title: 'Choose peace over winning', text: 'When their shepherds quarrel, Abram, the elder, lets Lot choose the land first: “Let there be no strife between you and me, for we are kinsmen.” Keeping the family together mattered more than getting the best land.', source: 'Genesis 13:8–9' },
      { title: 'Trust counts', text: 'Old and childless, Abram is told to look at the stars: his children will be as many. “He trusted in the LORD, and He counted it as righteousness.” Believing in a promise you can’t see yet is a kind of goodness.', source: 'Genesis 15:5–6' },
    ],
    days: [
      { verse: 'Genesis 12:1', headline: 'Go for yourself.', text: '<i>Lech lecha</i> literally means “go for yourself,” or even “go to yourself.” Rashi reads it as “for your own good.” The Hasidic teachers read it as a journey inward: leaving old habits behind to find out who you’re meant to be.', try: 'Leave one old habit behind today, even for a day.' },
      { verse: 'Genesis 13:3', headline: 'Go back the way you came.', text: 'Coming back from Egypt a rich man, Abram retraces his route “to the place where his tent had been at first.” Rashi says he stayed at the same inns as on the way down, and paid off the debts he’d run up. Success is a good time to go back and settle up.', try: 'Pay back a favor or a debt you’ve been meaning to.' },
      { verse: 'Genesis 13:14', headline: 'Lift up your eyes.', text: 'Right after Abram gives the best land to Lot, God tells him: “Lift up your eyes and look north and south, east and west. All the land you see I will give to you.” Letting go of the argument cleared his view.', try: 'Let someone else have the last word today.' },
      { verse: 'Genesis 14:13', headline: 'It’s fine to stand on the other side.', text: 'Here Abram is called “the Hebrew,” <i>Ivri</i>, from the word for “the other side.” A midrash says the whole world stood on one side and Abraham on the other (Bereshit Rabbah 42:8). Being in the minority isn’t a reason to stop.', try: 'Say what you actually think in one conversation today.' },
      { verse: 'Genesis 14:23', headline: 'Not even a sandal strap.', text: 'After winning the war, Abram refuses any reward from the king of Sodom, “not a thread or a sandal strap,” so no one can say, “I made Abram rich.” The Talmud says his children earned the threads of tzitzit and the straps of tefillin for this (Sotah 17a).', try: 'Do one good thing today and turn down the credit.' },
      { verse: 'Genesis 16:13', headline: 'You are seen.', text: 'Hagar, a servant who has run away, pregnant and alone in the desert, is the first person in the Torah to meet an angel. She gives God a name: “El-roi, the God who sees me.” The person everyone overlooked is the one who knows God is watching.', try: 'Really see one person today: ask how they are, and listen.' },
      { verse: 'Genesis 17:15', headline: 'From “my princess” to everyone’s.', text: 'God changes Sarai’s name to Sarah. Rashi explains: Sarai means “my princess,” a princess to Abraham alone; Sarah means a princess to the whole world. Her blessing grows from her family to everyone. What you have can be for more people than you think.', try: 'Share something you’ve been keeping to yourself.' },
    ],
  },

  'Vayera': {
    meaning: 'And He appeared',
    summary: 'God appears to Abraham as he welcomes three strangers, who announce that Sarah will have a son. Abraham argues with God to save the city of Sodom; it is destroyed, but his nephew Lot is rescued. Isaac is born, Hagar and Ishmael are sent away, and finally God tests Abraham by asking him to offer up Isaac, stopping him at the last moment.',
    lessons: [
      { title: 'Welcome the stranger', text: 'Still recovering from his circumcision, in the heat of the day, Abraham runs to greet three strangers and serves them a feast. The Talmud learns from here that welcoming guests is greater than receiving the Divine Presence.', source: 'Genesis 18:1–8 · Shabbat 127a' },
      { title: 'Argue for justice, even with God', text: '“Shall the Judge of all the earth not do justice?” Abraham bargains with God for Sodom, from fifty innocent people down to ten. Faith here isn’t silence. It’s caring enough to push back.', source: 'Genesis 18:23–32' },
      { title: 'Don’t look back', text: 'Lot’s wife looks back at burning Sodom and becomes a pillar of salt. Being rescued means walking forward; staring at what you’re losing can freeze you in place.', source: 'Genesis 19:26' },
      { title: '“Here I am”', text: 'Three times in the story of the binding of Isaac, Abraham answers <i>Hineni</i>, “here I am”: to God, to Isaac, and to the angel. Being fully there for each person who calls you is the heart of the story.', source: 'Genesis 22:1, 7, 11' },
    ],
    days: [
      { verse: 'Genesis 18:1', headline: 'Show up for someone who’s hurting.', text: 'The portion opens with God appearing to Abraham right after his circumcision. Rashi says God came to visit the sick. The Talmud says that’s why visiting the sick is a way of walking in God’s ways (Sotah 14a).', try: 'Call or visit someone who’s sick or having a hard time.' },
      { verse: 'Genesis 18:19', headline: 'Teach by how you live.', text: 'God explains why Abraham was chosen: so that he will teach his children “to keep the way of the LORD, doing what is right and just.” The first job of the first Jew is teaching the next generation, and children learn more from what we do than from what we say.', try: 'Do something kind where a younger person can see it.' },
      { verse: 'Genesis 19:16', headline: 'When you hesitate, let someone take your hand.', text: 'Told to run before Sodom is destroyed, Lot “lingered.” So the angels grab him, his wife, and his daughters by the hand and pull them out, “in the LORD’s mercy on him.” Sometimes we’re rescued not because we’re ready, but because someone won’t let go.', try: 'Accept help you’d normally wave off.' },
      { verse: 'Genesis 21:1', headline: 'Some promises take a long time.', text: '“The LORD remembered Sarah as He had said.” Twenty-five years after God first promised Abraham children, Isaac is born. Sarah once laughed at the idea; now she says, “God has given me laughter.” The thing you stopped believing in may still be on its way.', try: 'Pick back up something you gave up hoping for.' },
      { verse: 'Genesis 21:17', headline: 'You’re judged by who you are now.', text: 'Hagar and her son Ishmael are dying of thirst in the desert. God hears the boy “where he is.” Rashi explains: God judges a person by what they are right now, not by what they might become. Give people, and yourself, the same fairness.', try: 'Drop a grudge about something someone hasn’t even done yet.' },
      { verse: 'Genesis 21:33', headline: 'Plant something that feeds others.', text: 'Abraham plants an <i>eshel</i>, a tamarisk tree, in Beersheba. The Talmud says it was really an inn or an orchard for travelers, and after guests ate, Abraham taught them to thank God, not him (Sotah 10a). Kindness that points beyond itself lasts.', try: 'Feed someone today, or at least buy them a coffee.' },
      { verse: 'Genesis 22:8', headline: 'Walk together through what you don’t understand.', text: 'Isaac asks, “Where is the lamb?” Abraham answers, “God will see to it, my son.” And the Torah says, for the second time, “the two of them walked on together.” They don’t understand what’s happening, but they don’t let go of each other.', try: 'Stay beside someone in a hard moment, even without answers.' },
    ],
  },

  'Chayei Sara': {
    meaning: 'The life of Sarah',
    summary: 'Sarah dies at 127, and Abraham buys the Cave of Machpelah in Hebron to bury her: the first land the family owns in Canaan. He sends his servant to find a wife for Isaac. At a well, Rebecca’s kindness to the servant and his camels shows she’s the one. Isaac marries Rebecca and is comforted after his mother’s death. Abraham dies and is buried by his sons Isaac and Ishmael together.',
    lessons: [
      { title: 'Kindness is the real test', text: 'Abraham’s servant asks for a sign: the right woman will offer water to him and to his ten camels too. Rebecca does more than asked, running back and forth to the well. Character shows in what we do beyond the minimum.', source: 'Genesis 24:14–20' },
      { title: 'Honor the dead', text: 'Abraham insists on paying full price, in public, for a place to bury Sarah. Caring for the dead, who can never repay us, is what Jewish tradition calls <i>chesed shel emet</i>: true kindness.', source: 'Genesis 23:3–16' },
      { title: 'Love that heals', text: '“Isaac brought her into his mother Sarah’s tent… and he loved her, and Isaac was comforted after his mother.” The first time the Torah says a husband loved his wife, the love comes with healing from grief.', source: 'Genesis 24:67' },
      { title: 'Families can come back together', text: 'When Abraham dies, “Isaac and Ishmael his sons buried him.” The brothers, separated since childhood, stand together at their father’s grave. Rashi notes that Ishmael let Isaac go first: a sign he had changed.', source: 'Genesis 25:9 · Rashi' },
    ],
    days: [
      { verse: 'Genesis 23:1', headline: 'Every year of your life counts.', text: 'The Torah counts Sarah’s life oddly: “a hundred years and twenty years and seven years.” Rashi says each stage was whole: at 100 she was as free of sin as at 20, and at 20 as lovely as at 7. A portion called “the life of Sarah” begins with her death, because a life is measured by all its days.', try: 'Write down one good thing from each decade of your life.' },
      { verse: 'Genesis 24:1', headline: 'Live every day, not just a lot of them.', text: '“Abraham was old, well along in days, and the LORD had blessed Abraham in everything.” The mystics read “along in days” as “he came with his days,” with none of them wasted. It isn’t about how many days you get, but how many you really show up for.', try: 'Make today one you’ll remember.' },
      { verse: 'Genesis 24:12', headline: 'Ask for help, then watch for it.', text: 'At the well, Abraham’s servant prays for success that very day. Before he finishes speaking, Rebecca appears. He asked for a clear sign, kindness to a stranger, and he kept his eyes open to see it when it came.', try: 'Ask for help with one thing today, out loud.' },
      { verse: 'Genesis 24:33', headline: 'Do the job before you eat.', text: 'Offered a meal after a long trip, Abraham’s servant says, “I will not eat until I have said what I came to say.” He’s tired and hungry, but he finishes his mission first. Then he tells the whole story, giving credit to everyone involved.', try: 'Do the important thing before the comfortable one.' },
      { verse: 'Genesis 24:58', headline: 'Say yes to your own life.', text: 'Rebecca’s family wants her to wait, so they ask her. “Will you go with this man?” She answers in one Hebrew word: <i>Elech</i>, “I will go.” Rashi learns from here that a woman may not be married off without her consent.', try: 'Make one decision for yourself today instead of waiting.' },
      { verse: 'Genesis 25:8', headline: 'Contented is a good way to finish.', text: 'Abraham dies “at a good old age, old and satisfied.” The man who left everything, waited decades for a son, and faced terrible tests ends his life content. Satisfaction isn’t having had an easy life. It’s having lived the one you were given.', try: 'Before bed, list what you’re satisfied with today.' },
      { verse: 'Genesis 25:16', headline: 'Other people’s blessings are real too.', text: 'The portion ends by listing Ishmael’s twelve sons, princes of twelve tribes, just as God promised Abraham. The Torah keeps its promise to the son who wasn’t chosen, too. Someone else’s success doesn’t take away from yours.', try: 'Congratulate someone sincerely today.' },
    ],
  },

  'Toldot': {
    meaning: 'Generations',
    summary: 'After twenty years of waiting, Rebecca gives birth to twins, Esau and Jacob, who struggle even before they’re born. Esau sells his birthright to Jacob for a bowl of stew. Isaac re-digs his father’s wells and makes peace with the Philistines. When Isaac, old and blind, plans to bless Esau, Rebecca and Jacob trick him into blessing Jacob instead, and Jacob has to flee his furious brother.',
    lessons: [
      { title: 'Different children, different paths', text: 'Esau is a hunter, a man of the field; Jacob is a quiet man who stays among the tents. Each parent loves a different son. “Teach a child according to his way” (Proverbs 22:6): each person needs their own path.', source: 'Genesis 25:27–28' },
      { title: 'Don’t trade the future for right now', text: 'Hungry from the field, Esau sells his birthright for a bowl of lentil stew: “I’m about to die anyway, so what good is a birthright to me?” What we want this minute can crowd out what matters most.', source: 'Genesis 25:29–34' },
      { title: 'Re-dig the old wells', text: 'Isaac reopens the wells his father Abraham dug, which the Philistines had filled in, and gives them the same names his father had. Sometimes progress means recovering what an earlier generation already found.', source: 'Genesis 26:18' },
      { title: 'Deception has a cost', text: 'Jacob gets the blessing by disguise, and he pays for it: years away from home, and later he is tricked himself. The Torah doesn’t hide its heroes’ mistakes. It shows how they echo.', source: 'Genesis 27' },
    ],
    days: [
      { verse: 'Genesis 25:22', headline: 'Ask the question out loud.', text: 'Rebecca’s pregnancy is painful, and she asks, “If so, why do I exist?” Then she goes to ask God. She doesn’t hide her confusion or pretend things are fine. The answer, that two nations are inside her, explains the struggle without removing it.', try: 'Bring one hard question to someone you trust.' },
      { verse: 'Genesis 26:12', headline: 'Plant in the hard years.', text: 'There’s a famine, and Isaac plants anyway. That year he reaps a hundred times what he sowed. Of the three patriarchs, he’s the one who stays put and works the land. Steady effort right where you are is also faith.', try: 'Put some effort into something that won’t pay off for months.' },
      { verse: 'Genesis 26:22', headline: 'Keep digging until there’s room.', text: 'Isaac’s servants dig a well, and the locals fight over it. They dig another: more fighting. Isaac doesn’t fight. He moves on and digs a third, and no one quarrels. He names it Rehovot, “wide spaces”: “Now the LORD has made room for us.”', try: 'Walk away from one argument that isn’t worth it.' },
      { verse: 'Genesis 26:24', headline: 'You don’t have to be afraid.', text: 'After all the fighting over wells, God appears to Isaac at night: “Fear not, for I am with you.” Right after this, his enemies come to make peace. Calm often has to come first on the inside before it shows up on the outside.', try: 'When worry hits today, take three slow breaths.' },
      { verse: 'Genesis 27:22', headline: 'Make your voice and your hands match.', text: 'Blind Isaac touches the disguised Jacob and says: “The voice is the voice of Jacob, but the hands are the hands of Esau.” Something doesn’t add up. Integrity means what you say and what you do belong to the same person.', try: 'Find one place where your words and actions don’t match, and fix it.' },
      { verse: 'Genesis 27:38', headline: 'Hear the one who lost.', text: 'When Esau finds the blessing is gone, he cries out, “Do you have only one blessing, Father? Bless me too!” and weeps. The Torah makes us feel the pain of the brother who lost. Winning doesn’t make the other side’s tears unreal.', try: 'Be gentle with someone who lost out today.' },
      { verse: 'Genesis 28:9', headline: 'It’s never too late to try.', text: 'Esau finally notices that his Canaanite wives upset his parents, so he marries a daughter of Ishmael, from his father’s own family. It’s a small, late step, but a real one: the son who lost the blessing is still trying to be a good son.', try: 'Do one thing that would make your parents (or their memory) proud.' },
    ],
  },

  'Vayetzei': {
    meaning: 'And he went out',
    summary: 'Running from Esau, Jacob dreams of a ladder reaching to heaven with angels going up and down it, and God promises to protect him. In Haran he falls in love with Rachel and works seven years for her, but her father Laban tricks him into marrying her sister Leah first. Jacob marries both, and over twenty years his family grows to eleven sons and a daughter. Finally he leaves with his family and flocks, and makes peace with Laban.',
    lessons: [
      { title: 'God was here, and I didn’t know it', text: 'Waking from his dream of the ladder, Jacob says, “Surely the LORD is in this place, and I did not know it!” Holiness can turn up anywhere, even on a rock you’re sleeping on while running from home.', source: 'Genesis 28:16' },
      { title: 'Love makes time fly', text: 'Jacob works seven years for Rachel, “and they seemed to him like a few days, because of his love for her.” What we do out of love doesn’t feel like a burden.', source: 'Genesis 29:20' },
      { title: 'Saying thank you', text: 'When her fourth son is born, Leah names him Judah: “This time I will thank the LORD.” The Talmud says no one had thanked God like this before Leah. Jews, <i>Yehudim</i>, are named for Judah: people of thanks.', source: 'Genesis 29:35 · Berakhot 7b' },
      { title: 'Honest work', text: 'Jacob tells Laban: “By day the heat consumed me, and the frost by night, and sleep fled from my eyes.” He worked faithfully for a boss who cheated him. Maimonides holds him up as the model of how to do a job.', source: 'Genesis 31:40 · Rambam, Laws of Hiring 13:7' },
    ],
    days: [
      { verse: 'Genesis 28:12', headline: 'Go up, then come back down.', text: 'Jacob dreams of a ladder standing on the ground with its top in heaven, and angels “going up and coming down.” Up first, then down: the angels start here, on earth. Rising higher matters, but so does bringing what you learn back down to the ground.', try: 'Take one big idea and turn it into one small action.' },
      { verse: 'Genesis 29:10', headline: 'Love finds strength you didn’t know you had.', text: 'Shepherds wait for everyone to gather before moving the heavy stone off the well. Jacob sees Rachel and rolls it off by himself. Then he kisses her and bursts into tears. The Torah’s most emotional man meets the love of his life.', try: 'Do something hard today for someone you love.' },
      { verse: 'Genesis 29:31', headline: 'God sees the one who feels unloved.', text: '“The LORD saw that Leah was unloved.” Married to a man who loves her sister more, Leah names her sons after her pain and her hope: “Now my husband will love me.” God notices what Jacob doesn’t.', try: 'Pay attention to someone who often gets overlooked.' },
      { verse: 'Genesis 30:22', headline: 'Waiting isn’t being forgotten.', text: 'After years of watching her sister have children, “God remembered Rachel.” Rashi says God remembered how Rachel gave Leah the secret signs so she wouldn’t be shamed at the wedding. Generosity in your hardest moment is remembered.', try: 'Save someone from embarrassment today.' },
      { verse: 'Genesis 31:3', headline: 'Know when it’s time to go home.', text: 'After twenty years, Jacob sees that Laban isn’t friendly anymore, and God tells him: “Return to the land of your fathers, and I will be with you.” Before he goes, Jacob talks it over with Rachel and Leah, and they decide together.', try: 'Talk a big decision through with someone it affects.' },
      { verse: 'Genesis 31:19', headline: 'Decide what you’re taking with you.', text: 'Before leaving, Rachel takes her father’s household idols. Rashi says she wanted to wean Laban off idol worship. Whatever her reason, leaving home means deciding what to carry with you and what to leave behind.', try: 'Get rid of one thing that keeps pulling you back to an old habit.' },
      { verse: 'Genesis 32:2', headline: 'Angels at the border.', text: 'As Jacob leaves Laban, angels of God meet him, just as angels went up and down the ladder when he first left home. The portion begins and ends with angels. Every big transition is guarded, even when it doesn’t feel that way.', try: 'Notice who has your back as you start something new.' },
    ],
  },

  'Vayishlach': {
    meaning: 'And he sent',
    summary: 'Jacob prepares to meet Esau after twenty years, sending gifts and praying for help. The night before, he wrestles with a mysterious man until dawn and is given a new name: Israel. The brothers meet and embrace in tears. Jacob’s daughter Dinah is assaulted in Shechem, and her brothers take violent revenge. Rachel dies giving birth to Benjamin, and Isaac dies, buried by both of his sons.',
    lessons: [
      { title: 'Prepare every way you can', text: 'Facing Esau, Jacob sends gifts, prays, and gets his camp ready to defend itself. Rashi says he prepared three ways: with a gift, with prayer, and for battle. Wise people don’t rely on just one approach.', source: 'Genesis 32:4–22 · Rashi' },
      { title: 'Wrestle with it', text: 'Jacob wrestles all night and won’t let go until he’s blessed. His new name, Israel, means one who “struggled with God and with people, and won.” Struggle isn’t the opposite of faith. For Israel, it’s the name.', source: 'Genesis 32:25–29' },
      { title: '“I am too small”', text: 'Before asking God for anything, Jacob prays: “I am too small for all the kindness and faithfulness You have shown me.” Humility and gratitude come first.', source: 'Genesis 32:11' },
      { title: 'People can make up', text: 'Esau, who once swore to kill Jacob, runs to meet him, hugs him, kisses him, and they both cry. Twenty years of anger end in an embrace. No rift is too old to heal.', source: 'Genesis 33:4' },
    ],
    days: [
      { verse: 'Genesis 32:8', headline: 'Be afraid of hurting others too.', text: '“Jacob was very frightened and distressed.” Rashi explains the double phrase: frightened he might be killed, distressed that he might have to kill. A good person in a fight worries not only about losing, but about what winning might cost.', try: 'Before your next disagreement, think about how to win without wounding.' },
      { verse: 'Genesis 32:25', headline: 'Alone, and still wrestling.', text: '“Jacob was left alone, and a man wrestled with him until daybreak.” The Talmud says he had gone back across the river for some small jars he’d left behind (Chullin 91a). Alone in the dark, over small things, he faces the struggle that gives him his name.', try: 'Face one thing you’ve been avoiding.' },
      { verse: 'Genesis 32:32', headline: 'You can be blessed and limping.', text: 'As the sun comes up, Jacob walks away from the fight with a new name, and limping on his hip. Both are true at once. We often come out of our hardest struggles stronger and still carrying a wound.', try: 'Be patient with a wound that’s still healing, yours or someone else’s.' },
      { verse: 'Genesis 33:11', headline: '“I have plenty” or “I have everything”?', text: 'Esau turns down Jacob’s gift: “I have plenty.” Jacob insists: “Please take it, for God has been kind to me, and I have everything.” Plenty can always grow; everything means enough. One brother counts what he has, the other is content with it.', try: 'Give something away from a feeling of “I have enough.”' },
      { verse: 'Genesis 35:2', headline: 'Clean house before you start again.', text: 'After the violence at Shechem, Jacob tells his family: get rid of the foreign idols you have, wash, and change your clothes; then we’ll go up to Beth-El and build an altar. A fresh start begins with letting go of what you’ve been carrying.', try: 'Clear one cluttered space: a drawer, an inbox, or a grudge.' },
      { verse: 'Genesis 35:19', headline: 'Someone is waiting on the road.', text: 'Rachel dies giving birth to Benjamin and is buried by the road to Bethlehem, not in the family cave. Jeremiah later pictures her weeping for her children as they go into exile, and God promising they will come home (Jeremiah 31). She waits on the road for everyone who wanders.', try: 'Pray, or hope hard, for someone far from home.' },
      { verse: 'Genesis 36:31', headline: 'Slow doesn’t mean behind.', text: 'Esau’s family has chiefs and kings “before any king ruled over the Israelites.” Jacob’s family takes centuries longer to get there. The Torah lists Esau’s success quickly and moves on; the slower story is the one it tells at length.', try: 'Stop comparing your timeline to someone else’s today.' },
    ],
  },

  'Vayeshev': {
    meaning: 'And he settled',
    summary: 'Jacob gives his favorite son Joseph a special coat, and Joseph’s dreams of ruling over his brothers make them hate him. They throw him into a pit and sell him into slavery, then tell Jacob a wild animal killed him. Judah learns a hard lesson from his daughter-in-law Tamar. In Egypt, Joseph rises in Potiphar’s house, refuses Potiphar’s wife, and is thrown into prison, where he explains the dreams of Pharaoh’s butler and baker.',
    lessons: [
      { title: 'Favoritism hurts', text: 'Jacob loves Joseph most and gives him a fancy coat, and his brothers “could not speak a kind word to him.” The Talmud warns parents never to single out one child: a little fine wool led to slavery in Egypt.', source: 'Genesis 37:3–4 · Shabbat 10b' },
      { title: 'Own your mistakes', text: 'When Tamar shows that Judah is the father of her child, he says, “She is more right than I.” Admitting he was wrong, in public, is what makes Judah a leader.', source: 'Genesis 38:26' },
      { title: 'Say no, even unwatched', text: 'Potiphar’s wife pressures Joseph day after day. He refuses: “How could I do this great wrong and sin against God?” Integrity is who you are when it would be easy not to be.', source: 'Genesis 39:9–10' },
      { title: 'God is with you in the pit', text: 'As a slave and as a prisoner, “the LORD was with Joseph.” His circumstances keep falling apart; his character keeps lifting him up. Where you are isn’t who you are.', source: 'Genesis 39:2, 21' },
    ],
    days: [
      { verse: 'Genesis 37:4', headline: 'At least be honest about it.', text: 'Joseph’s brothers “could not speak a peaceful word to him.” Rashi finds something to praise even here: they didn’t fake friendliness while hating him inside. Being honest about bad feelings is where fixing them starts. Pretending blocks the way.', try: 'Tell someone honestly, and kindly, how you feel.' },
      { verse: 'Genesis 37:15', headline: 'One question can change everything.', text: 'Joseph gets lost looking for his brothers, and a nameless man asks him, “What are you looking for?” Without that stranger, Joseph would have gone home, and the whole story, Egypt and the rescue from famine, never happens. Small encounters can change everything.', try: 'Ask someone who seems lost if they need help.' },
      { verse: 'Genesis 37:24', headline: 'Empty isn’t the same as safe.', text: '“The pit was empty; there was no water in it.” Why say both? The Talmud answers: there was no water, but there were snakes and scorpions (Shabbat 22a). An empty space in a life rarely stays empty. If you don’t fill it with something good, something else moves in.', try: 'Fill one empty hour today with something good.' },
      { verse: 'Genesis 38:25', headline: 'Never shame someone in public.', text: 'About to be put to death, Tamar could prove that Judah is the father. Instead she quietly sends him his own seal: “Please recognize whose these are.” The Talmud learns: better to be thrown into a furnace than to embarrass someone in public (Sotah 10b).', try: 'If you need to correct someone today, do it privately.' },
      { verse: 'Genesis 39:3', headline: 'Let people see what you lean on.', text: 'Even his Egyptian master can tell “the LORD was with him.” How? Rashi says the name of God was always on Joseph’s lips. He didn’t hide what kept him going, and it showed in how well he did his work.', try: 'Thank God, or say “thank goodness,” out loud for something.' },
      { verse: 'Genesis 39:10', headline: 'Temptation asks every day.', text: 'Potiphar’s wife pressures Joseph “day after day,” and every day he says no. Saying no once is a decision; saying no every day is a character. Jewish tradition calls him <i>Yosef HaTzaddik</i>, Joseph the righteous, for exactly this.', try: 'Say no to one small temptation today, and again tomorrow.' },
      { verse: 'Genesis 40:7', headline: 'Notice the face in front of you.', text: 'In prison, Joseph sees Pharaoh’s butler and baker looking troubled and asks, “Why do you look so sad today?” His own life is a mess, but he notices theirs. That one caring question leads, two years later, to his freedom.', try: 'Ask someone who looks down how they’re really doing.' },
    ],
  },

  'Miketz': {
    meaning: 'At the end',
    summary: 'Two years later, Pharaoh dreams of seven fat cows eaten by seven thin ones. Joseph, brought up from prison, explains: seven years of plenty are coming, then seven of famine. Pharaoh puts him in charge of all Egypt. When the famine comes, Joseph’s brothers come to buy food. He recognizes them, but they don’t recognize him, and he tests them, finally framing Benjamin with a stolen silver cup.',
    lessons: [
      { title: '“Not me. God.”', text: 'Asked to explain Pharaoh’s dream, Joseph answers, “Not I; God will give Pharaoh an answer.” Talent is real, but humble people know where it comes from.', source: 'Genesis 41:16' },
      { title: 'Save in the good years', text: 'Joseph’s plan is simple: store food during the seven good years to get through the seven bad ones. Wisdom is preparing for hard times while times are still good.', source: 'Genesis 41:33–36' },
      { title: 'Guilt can lead to change', text: 'Accused of spying, the brothers say to each other: “We are being punished because of our brother. We saw his distress when he pleaded with us, and we wouldn’t listen.” More than twenty years later, they finally say it out loud.', source: 'Genesis 42:21' },
      { title: 'Taking responsibility', text: 'Judah tells his father, “I will be responsible for him; you can hold me accountable.” The brother who once sold Joseph now puts himself on the line for Rachel’s other son, Benjamin.', source: 'Genesis 43:9' },
    ],
    days: [
      { verse: 'Genesis 41:14', headline: 'Everything can change in a day.', text: 'After years in prison, it happens fast: “they rushed him out of the dungeon.” Joseph shaves, changes his clothes, and stands before the most powerful man on earth. Jewish tradition says rescue can come “in the blink of an eye.” Stay ready.', try: 'Get ready for an opportunity before it shows up.' },
      { verse: 'Genesis 41:38', headline: 'Character gets noticed.', text: 'Pharaoh, a king who worships other gods, looks at a Hebrew slave and says: “Could we find another like him, a man with the spirit of God in him?” Joseph’s wisdom and honesty speak for themselves, even to people who don’t share his faith.', try: 'Do your work today as if your character is on display. It is.' },
      { verse: 'Genesis 41:52', headline: 'Bloom where it hurt.', text: 'Joseph names his second son Ephraim: “God has made me fruitful in the land of my suffering.” He doesn’t pretend Egypt wasn’t painful, and he doesn’t let the pain be the whole story. Both things stay true.', try: 'Name one good thing that grew out of a hard time.' },
      { verse: 'Genesis 42:8', headline: 'People change more than you think.', text: '“Joseph recognized his brothers, but they did not recognize him.” Rashi says Joseph left as a boy without a beard and now had one. The brothers still see the dreamer they sold. Are you letting someone grow, or still seeing who they used to be?', try: 'Give someone a chance to be different from how you remember them.' },
      { verse: 'Genesis 43:14', headline: 'Let go of what you can’t control.', text: 'Jacob finally lets Benjamin go to Egypt, saying: “May God Almighty give you mercy… As for me, if I must lose my children, I will lose them.” He has done all he can. What happens next isn’t his to hold.', try: 'Do your part on one worry today, then let it go.' },
      { verse: 'Genesis 43:29', headline: 'Bless the person in front of you.', text: 'Joseph sees his little brother Benjamin for the first time in over twenty years. He can’t reveal himself yet, so he says what he can: “God be gracious to you, my son.” Then he hurries out of the room to cry. Sometimes a blessing is all we can give.', try: 'Give someone a sincere blessing or compliment today.' },
      { verse: 'Genesis 44:16', headline: 'Stand together when it gets hard.', text: 'When the cup is found in Benjamin’s bag, the brothers don’t abandon him. They tear their clothes and all go back together, and Judah says, “God has found out our guilt.” Years ago they left a brother behind. This time, they don’t.', try: 'Stand by someone today who’s in trouble.' },
    ],
  },

  'Vayigash': {
    meaning: 'And he came close',
    summary: 'Judah steps forward and offers to stay as a slave in Benjamin’s place. Unable to hold back any longer, Joseph reveals himself: “I am Joseph.” He forgives his brothers and sends for his father. Jacob, told that Joseph is alive, comes down to Egypt with the whole family, seventy people. Father and son are reunited, and the family settles in Goshen while Joseph runs Egypt through the famine.',
    lessons: [
      { title: 'Step forward', text: 'Judah approaches the most powerful man in Egypt and offers himself instead of Benjamin. Real repentance, Maimonides says, is facing the same situation again and choosing differently. That’s exactly what Judah does.', source: 'Genesis 44:18–34 · Rambam, Laws of Repentance 2:1' },
      { title: 'Find a purpose in the past', text: 'Joseph tells his brothers: “Don’t be upset that you sold me here, for God sent me ahead of you to save lives.” He doesn’t erase what they did, but he finds a purpose in it.', source: 'Genesis 45:5' },
      { title: 'Don’t fight on the way', text: 'Sending his brothers home, Joseph says, “Don’t quarrel on the way.” Families slip back into old blame easily. Peace has to be protected, especially right after it’s made.', source: 'Genesis 45:24' },
      { title: 'The heart needs time', text: 'Told that Joseph is alive, Jacob’s “heart went numb, for he did not believe them.” Only when he sees the wagons Joseph sent does his spirit come back to life. Good news can take time to sink in.', source: 'Genesis 45:26–27' },
    ],
    days: [
      { verse: 'Genesis 44:18', headline: 'Come close before you speak.', text: '<i>Vayigash</i>: “and Judah came close.” He doesn’t shout across the room. He steps up and speaks directly, respectfully, and from the heart about his father. Hard conversations go better up close.', try: 'Have one important conversation in person instead of by text.' },
      { verse: 'Genesis 45:3', headline: 'Two words can end a long silence.', text: '“I am Joseph. Is my father still alive?” After twenty-two years, the brothers are too stunned to answer. The Talmud says Rabbi Elazar wept at this verse: if they couldn’t answer Joseph, how will we answer when our own lives are reviewed? (Chagigah 4b)', try: 'Say the honest thing you’ve been holding back.' },
      { verse: 'Genesis 45:15', headline: 'After the tears, you can talk.', text: 'Joseph kisses each brother and cries, “and after that his brothers talked with him.” When they were young they couldn’t say a kind word to him. Real conversation came only after he showed them, with tears, that he loved them.', try: 'Show someone you care before you try to solve the problem.' },
      { verse: 'Genesis 45:22', headline: 'Watch out for old patterns.', text: 'Joseph gives each brother a change of clothes, but Benjamin gets five, plus 300 pieces of silver. The Talmud is startled: would Joseph, who suffered from his father’s favoritism, repeat it? (Megillah 16a). Even good people can slip into the patterns that once hurt them.', try: 'Notice one habit you picked up from your family. Keep it or drop it on purpose.' },
      { verse: 'Genesis 46:3', headline: 'Going down can be the way up.', text: 'Old Jacob is afraid to leave the promised land. God reassures him: “Don’t be afraid to go down to Egypt, for I will make you a great nation there. I will go down with you, and I will bring you back up.” Not every step down is a defeat.', try: 'Look for what a setback today might be preparing you for.' },
      { verse: 'Genesis 46:29', headline: 'Make room for what you love most.', text: 'Joseph rushes to meet his father and weeps on his neck for a long time. Rashi says Jacob didn’t weep back: he was saying the Shema. After twenty-two years of grief, the first thing Jacob does with his overflowing love is turn it toward God.', try: 'Turn one moment of joy today into a thank-you.' },
      { verse: 'Genesis 47:12', headline: 'Feed the people who once hurt you.', text: 'Joseph provides food for his father, his brothers, and all their children: the same brothers who sold him. Forgiveness here isn’t a speech. It’s bread, every day, for years. That’s what makes it real.', try: 'Do something practical for someone you’ve forgiven.' },
    ],
  },

  'Vayechi': {
    meaning: 'And he lived',
    summary: 'Jacob lives his last seventeen years in Egypt. He asks Joseph to bury him in Canaan, blesses Joseph’s sons Ephraim and Manasseh (putting the younger first), and gathers his twelve sons to bless each one. After Jacob dies, the brothers fear Joseph will finally take revenge, but he comforts them. The book of Genesis ends with Joseph’s death and his request that his bones be carried to the promised land.',
    lessons: [
      { title: 'Kindness that can’t be repaid', text: 'Jacob asks Joseph to bury him in Canaan with “kindness and truth.” Rashi explains: kindness to the dead is true kindness, because the one who does it expects nothing back.', source: 'Genesis 47:29 · Rashi' },
      { title: 'Bless your children', text: 'Jacob blesses Ephraim and Manasseh, and to this day parents bless their sons on Friday night: “May God make you like Ephraim and Manasseh.” They were the first brothers in Genesis who didn’t fight.', source: 'Genesis 48:20' },
      { title: 'Everyone gets their own blessing', text: 'Jacob blesses each of his twelve sons differently, “each according to his own blessing.” No two people have the same gifts, and no two need the same encouragement.', source: 'Genesis 49:28' },
      { title: 'Harm can be turned to good', text: 'Joseph tells his frightened brothers: “Am I in the place of God? You meant evil against me, but God meant it for good.” His forgiveness comes from humility: it isn’t his place to get even.', source: 'Genesis 50:19–20' },
    ],
    days: [
      { verse: 'Genesis 47:28', headline: 'The best years can come late.', text: '“Jacob lived in the land of Egypt seventeen years.” Commentators notice this matches the seventeen years Joseph lived at home before he was sold. After all his suffering, Jacob’s last years, surrounded by his whole family, may have been his best.', try: 'Spend time with an older relative or friend.' },
      { verse: 'Genesis 48:11', headline: 'More than you hoped for.', text: 'Nearly blind, Jacob holds Joseph’s sons and says: “I never expected to see your face again, and now God has let me see your children too.” Sometimes life gives back more than it took.', try: 'Notice a blessing in your life you once thought was impossible.' },
      { verse: 'Genesis 48:19', headline: '“I know, my son, I know.”', text: 'Joseph thinks his father is confused when he crosses his hands to bless the younger grandson first. Jacob answers gently: “I know, my son, I know.” Old age isn’t confusion. Sometimes the older person sees what the younger can’t.', try: 'Ask an older person for advice, and really listen.' },
      { verse: 'Genesis 49:1', headline: 'You don’t need to know the ending.', text: 'Jacob calls his sons: “Gather, and I’ll tell you what will happen at the end of days.” Then he doesn’t. Rashi says he wanted to reveal the future, but God’s presence left him. Instead he gives each son a blessing for now. That’s usually what we get, too.', try: 'Do today’s work without needing to know how it all turns out.' },
      { verse: 'Genesis 49:22', headline: 'Grow by the water.', text: 'Jacob calls Joseph “a fruitful vine by a spring, whose branches climb over the wall.” A plant near water keeps growing no matter what’s thrown at it. Find what keeps you nourished, and stay close to it.', try: 'Spend time today with whatever refills you.' },
      { verse: 'Genesis 50:17', headline: 'Ask for forgiveness, even late.', text: 'After Jacob dies, the brothers fear Joseph was only kind for their father’s sake. They send a message asking forgiveness, and Joseph weeps. Seventeen years after he forgave them, they finally ask, and it hurts him that they still doubted him.', try: 'Apologize for something, even if it’s been a long time.' },
      { verse: 'Genesis 50:25', headline: 'Plan to go home.', text: 'Genesis ends in Egypt, with a coffin. But Joseph’s last words are a promise, “God will surely remember you,” and he makes his family swear to carry his bones home. The book ends far from home, looking toward the future.', try: 'Write down one hope you’re carrying into the next chapter.' },
    ],
  },
});
